  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nSynopsis: A deep upper trough is shown by all guidance to move \nthrough the central CONUS on Tuesday, resulting in a high \nconfidence in precip chances (90+%) moving west to east through \nthe area. There still remains considerable model spread on some\ndetails of the system, such as shortwave energy\nlocation/strength within the larger trough and subsequent low\nlevel pressure fields. Cluster analysis of ensembles doesn't\nsignificantly favor one outcome over another, further reducing\nconfidence on outcomes such as wind gusts and precip amounts.\nMid to late week is considerably uncertain as models\nsignificantly vary in the evolution of the pattern, with some\nshowing the trough or upper low stalling and lingering near the\nCWA, while others have a more progressive pattern with shortwave\nenergy moving through. \n\nWinds Monday: Minimally hazardous southerly gusts up to 35 mph \nare currently expected for Monday. Given the uncertainty in the\nlow level pressure fields, these expectations may change with\ntime, but current NBM probabilities for gusts above Wind \nAdvisory levels are generally < 10%. \n\nSevere potential late Monday into Monday night: SPC has a\nmarginal risk for severe weather along/West of Hwy 65 and a\nslight risk over portions of our southeast Kansas counties. It\nis quite likely that deep layer shear will be > 50 kts, but\ninstability and other near-term details remain quite uncertain.\nAt this time, ensemble and deterministic outputs keep MUCAPE < \n500 J/kg, with many showing < 250 J/kg. This limits confidence\nin severe potential in our CWA, but many of the important \nmesoscale details are not resolvable given the uncertainty in\neven synoptic level details.\n\nRainfall amounts late Monday and Tuesday: Overall amounts have\nbeen trending lower, with the forecast totals now at 0.8-1.5\ninches. While considerable uncertainly in these details remains,\nCAM output suggests that some areas have potential to see very\nlittle rainfall and many areas may not see meaningful drought\nrelief. NBM probabilities for total rainfall >= 1.0 inches\nranges from 50% over the western CWA to 70% over the east.\nProbabilities for >= 2.0 inches is 10-40% (greatest SE of\nSpringfield). Not seeing any noteworthy flooding potential at\nthis time.\n\nMid to late week: Given uncertainty, kept the NBM blend\nuntouched in this time range, resulting some 15-25% chances for\nrain, highs in the 60s, and lows in the 30s to 40s.",
